Social insurance, compulsory insurance and the state
The nineteenth century also changed the relationship between insurance and the state. Earlier insurance was largely voluntary and private or mutual. Industrialisation, urbanisation and wage labour created risks that governments increasingly treated as social rather than purely individual problems.
Germany under Bismarck is the canonical case. Between 1883 and 1889, the German state introduced major compulsory social-insurance schemes covering sickness, industrial accidents, invalidity and old age (International Labour Organization 2009). These programmes combined compulsory participation, contributions from workers and employers, and state regulation.
The twentieth century expanded this logic enormously. Depending on the country, states developed pensions, unemployment protection, health systems and workers’ compensation; they also made private insurance compulsory in areas such as motor third-party liability or professional liability.
The modern state can therefore occupy several roles at once:

regulator, imposing solvency, conduct and reserving rules;
insurer, through social-insurance programmes;
buyer of insurance, for its own assets and activities;
mandator, requiring individuals or firms to buy specified cover;
backstop or reinsurer of last resort, especially for risks that private markets cannot easily absorb alone.

Modern insurance is thus neither purely private nor purely public. It is a layered system involving households, firms, mutuals, insurers, reinsurers, capital markets and governments. For very large or difficult-to-diversify risks – natural catastrophes, terrorism, war-related losses or systemic crises – states may also provide guarantees, public reinsurance or emergency compensation. The boundary between an insurable private risk and a social risk is therefore itself an institutional and political choice.
